Here's the formal **testsmells.org 19-Smell Academic Catalog** audit of your integration test suite.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 2           | 2              |
| Medium   | 2           | 2              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` | **Method:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 95)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Problem:** The `if/else` means one assertion path *never* runs. If `loaded` is always a base `User`, the discount-rate check is dead code and gives false confidence.

**Fix:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_ReturnsBaseUser()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetPremiumUser_HasDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("vip@example.com", "VIP", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("vip@example.com")!;

    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Hidden testing gap — one branch is likely never validated.

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` | **Method:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113)

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
}
```

**Problem:** No assertion at all. This passes even if every insert silently fails (e.g., a no-op stub). The test name implies "no exception thrown" but frameworks already fail on unhandled exceptions.

**Fix:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — 100 % pass rate with zero behavioral verification.

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM — Sleepy Test

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` | **Method:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);
```

**Problem:** Hard-coded 3-second sleep. Adds wall-clock time to the suite and is still non-deterministic (can fail if the system is slower than expected). *Severity downgraded from High because this class is marked `[TestCategory("Integration")]`* — integration tests may need async coordination, but `Thread.Sleep` is still the wrong tool.

**Fix:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    // Poll with timeout instead of blind sleep
    var sent = await WaitUntilAsync(
        () => _repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

    Assert.IsTrue(sent, "Notification was not sent within timeout.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky failures in CI; adds 3 s to every run.

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` | **Method:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (line 28)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

**Problem:** 4 assertions with no message parameters. On failure, the MSTest output shows only "Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<null>" — you must read the code to know *which property* was wrong.

**Fix:** Add descriptive messages:

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should be persisted");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should be persisted");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Id should be auto-generated");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower diagnosis when tests fail in CI pipelines.

---

## Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The following tests are well-designed:

| Method | Why it's good |
|--------|---------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange-act-assert, focused on one behavior |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single assertion on the expected outcome |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count matches items added (self-documenting), uses `CollectionAssert` |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Tests real concurrency concern with proper assertion |

The test class also correctly uses an **in-memory SQLite database** — this avoids the *Mystery Guest* smell entirely (no external state dependency), which is excellent practice for integration tests.

---

## Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Effort | Action |
|----------|-------|--------|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | 2 min | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, ...)` to `BulkInsert` |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | 5 min | Split into two focused test methods |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | 10 min | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with polling/retry helper |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | 3 min | Add message strings to multi-assert methods |

**Bottom line:** 4 out of 8 tests are clean. The two High-severity smells (#1 and #2 above) are quick fixes that will meaningfully improve test trustworthiness. Address those first.
